      "content": "<h3>What is a HashSet?</h3>\n<p>A <strong>HashSet</strong> is a collection that <strong>never holds the same element twice</strong>. Add \"Raigad\" a second time and nothing happens. It is built for speed: asking \"is this fort in the set?\" takes roughly the <strong>same tiny time</strong> whether the set holds ten forts or ten million.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Package <code>java.util</code>. Implements the <code>Set</code> interface (a <code>Collection</code> with <strong>no duplicates</strong>).</li>\n<li><strong>No index:</strong> a set has no <code>get(i)</code>. You ask whether something is in it, add it, remove it, or loop over it.</li>\n<li><strong>No guaranteed order:</strong> elements come out in an order decided by their hash codes. It can look random and can change when the set grows. If you need an order use <code>LinkedHashSet</code> (insertion order) or <code>TreeSet</code> (sorted).</li>\n<li><strong>One null</strong> is allowed.</li>\n<li><strong>Fast:</strong> <code>add</code>, <code>remove</code> and <code>contains</code> are O(1) on average.</li>\n<li><strong>Not thread-safe.</strong> Internally a HashSet is a <code>HashMap</code> whose keys are your elements.</li>\n<li>Your element class must implement <code>equals()</code> and <code>hashCode()</code> properly (String, Integer and records already do).</li>\n</ul>"
